// This source code is subject to the terms of the Mozilla Public License 2.0 at https://mozilla.org/MPL/2.0/
//@version=5

// =============================================================================
// NQ / Mega-Cap Basket Z-Score Pairs Signal
//
// PINE VERSION NOTE: written against //@version=5. Pine v6 was requested as
// the preferred target, but this environment has no live TradingView editor
// to compile/verify v6-specific syntax against, and the existing script in
// this repo (Momentum_ROC_Signal_ES_NQ.pine) is also v5 -- so v5 was chosen
// as the reliable, verifiable choice. Everything here (request.security,
// ta.*, table.*, plotshape location.absolute, force_overlay) is standard,
// stable Pine v5 syntax that should port to v6 with little or no change if
// you choose to upgrade the version pragma yourself.
//
// WHAT THIS IS: a faithful re-implementation, as a live/monitoring
// indicator, of one specific rule from an already-completed backtest run on
// SYNTHETIC daily data (2018-2026, train -> validate -> test methodology,
// 5 candidate index/basket pairs tested). Of those 5 pairs, NQ vs. an
// equal-weighted top-10 Nasdaq-100 mega-cap basket had the best win rate
// (42.9%) and the best Sharpe ratio / profit factor -- i.e. it was the
// *least bad* of the five. It still LOST MONEY net of transaction costs in
// the out-of-sample test window: -6.4% return, -17.8% max drawdown.
//
// THIS IS NOT A PROVEN PROFITABLE STRATEGY, INCLUDING ON THIS PAIR. It is
// published here strictly as a research / monitoring tool so the same
// mean-reversion rule can be watched live. Treat every signal as a
// hypothesis, not a recommendation. Paper-trade and re-validate against
// TradingView's own historical data (which will differ from the synthetic
// backtest data this was tuned on) before ever risking real capital, and
// re-run the companion strategy() script (see /strategies in this repo) to
// confirm the Train / Validation / Test performance split still resembles
// the original result before trusting this on a live account.
//
// CHART CONTEXT: designed to run on NQ1! (CME Nasdaq-100 E-mini continuous
// futures), Daily timeframe -- hedgeWindow/lookbackZ/entryZ/exitZ/stopZ were
// all tuned on daily bars. Loading this on a different symbol or a
// different timeframe (intraday, weekly, etc.) will produce a statistically
// different z-score process and the default thresholds will no longer be
// meaningful; re-tune (or at least re-validate) before relying on it
// anywhere but NQ1! Daily.
//
// NO-REPAINT DESIGN: beta/spread/zscore are recomputed every bar (including
// the live, still-forming bar), but every trading DECISION (entry, exit,
// stop) is deliberately evaluated one bar lagged -- i.e. off zscore[1], the
// z-score as of the *previous, fully confirmed* bar's close -- exactly like
// a rolling-window backtest that decides on bar T's close and can only act
// from bar T+1 onward. This means a signal, once plotted, never moves or
// disappears on a later recalculation. The basket/partner data itself is
// pulled with request.security(..., lookahead = barmerge.lookahead_off) for
// the same reason.
//
// ON-CHART ENTRY / TAKE-PROFIT / STOP-LOSS: the current open trade's entry,
// take-profit, and stop-loss are drawn both in the z-score pane (native
// units) AND, via force_overlay, directly on the NQ candles in price terms
// (see the NQ PRICE PROJECTION block). The NQ price entry is an actual
// historical fill price; the NQ price TP/SL are a LIVE estimate that
// re-solves the z-score formula for NQ price every bar (holding today's
// beta/spread stats fixed) -- they will visibly shift as those stats
// update, which is intentional and matches how the real exit/stop
// condition itself works (it's also re-evaluated from live data, never
// frozen at entry).
//
// PROP FIRM RISK MANAGEMENT: position size (in NQ or MNQ contracts) is
// computed at every prospective entry from the live implied stop distance,
// capped so a single trade risks no more than Risk Per Trade % of Max
// Account Loss. Realized NQ-leg $ P&L accumulates across closed trades; once
// cumulative losses reach -Max Account Loss, the kill switch latches
// permanently and no further entries are taken (flagged with a loud on-chart
// warning). This is a PLANNING aid, not a broker-enforced guarantee: it
// cannot place real stop orders or talk to your prop firm's platform, and a
// live stop can still gap past its projected price -- always place a real
// protective stop sized the same way this script suggests.
//
// INTRADAY ACCOMMODATION: two things specifically corrupt this signal on
// timeframes finer than Daily, and both have an opt-out toggle below.
// (1) The 10 basket stocks only trade NYSE/NASDAQ regular hours (09:30-
// 16:00 ET) while NQ trades nearly 24/5, so outside RTH request.security()
// is forward-filling a STALE stock price against a moving NQ price -- a
// pure session-mismatch artifact, not real divergence. The RTH Session
// Filter suppresses signal evaluation entirely outside that window. (2)
// hedgeWindow/lookbackZ = 90 means "90 daily bars" as tuned; left
// unscaled on intraday bars it becomes 90 bars of just a few hours,
// nowhere near the same statistical memory. Auto-Scale Windows for
// Intraday stretches both by the chart's estimated bars-per-RTH-day so
// they still span roughly 90 TRADING DAYS either way. Together these fix
// the specific failure mode that produced ~220 trades / ~18% win rate on
// a 5-minute chart during testing -- they do NOT mean intraday entry/exit
// threshold behavior has been separately validated, only that the two
// most obvious sources of artifact-driven false signals are addressed.
//
// TRADE HISTORY: a second table (bottom-right) logs the last N closed
// trades (direction, entry/exit price, $ P&L, win/loss/stop), most recent
// first -- an indicator has no native "List of Trades" tab the way a
// strategy() does, so this rolling log is built manually from an array.
// The companion strategy file gets the authoritative version of this for
// free via TradingView's own Strategy Tester panel.
// =============================================================================
indicator("NQ / Basket Z-Score Pairs Signal", shorttitle = "NQ/Basket Z", overlay = false, format = format.price)

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// INPUTS
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
grpPartner = "Partner Instrument"
grpBasket  = "Basket Constituents (used only when Partner = Equal-Weight Basket)"
grpStrat   = "Strategy Parameters (tuned on NQ1! Daily -- see header)"
grpSession = "Intraday Accommodation (only matters off the Daily chart)"
grpRisk    = "Prop Firm Risk Management"
grpDisplay = "Display"

partnerChoice = input.string("Equal-Weight Basket", "Partner Instrument", options = ["Equal-Weight Basket", "QQQ", "ES1!", "XLK"], group = grpPartner, tooltip = "Equal-Weight Basket = the 10-stock index built below. Any other choice fetches that single symbol via request.security() instead and the 10-stock basket is ignored for signal purposes.")

sym1  = input.symbol("AAPL",  "Constituent 1",  group = grpBasket)
sym2  = input.symbol("MSFT",  "Constituent 2",  group = grpBasket)
sym3  = input.symbol("NVDA",  "Constituent 3",  group = grpBasket)
sym4  = input.symbol("AMZN",  "Constituent 4",  group = grpBasket)
sym5  = input.symbol("META",  "Constituent 5",  group = grpBasket)
sym6  = input.symbol("GOOGL", "Constituent 6",  group = grpBasket)
sym7  = input.symbol("AVGO",  "Constituent 7",  group = grpBasket)
sym8  = input.symbol("TSLA",  "Constituent 8",  group = grpBasket)
sym9  = input.symbol("COST",  "Constituent 9",  group = grpBasket)
sym10 = input.symbol("NFLX",  "Constituent 10", group = grpBasket)

hedgeWindow = input.int(90, "Hedge Ratio Window (bars)", minval = 5, group = grpStrat, tooltip = "Rolling window used to derive the beta (hedge ratio) between log(partner) and log(NQ). Tuned as 90 daily bars -- rescale if you change timeframe.")
lookbackZ   = input.int(90, "Z-Score Lookback (bars)", minval = 5, group = grpStrat, tooltip = "Rolling window for the spread's mean/stdev used to compute the z-score. Tuned as 90 daily bars.")
entryZ      = input.float(2.0, "Entry Threshold |z|", minval = 0.1, step = 0.1, group = grpStrat, tooltip = "Flat -> position when |z| crosses beyond this level.")
exitZ       = input.float(0.5, "Exit-to-Flat Threshold |z|", minval = 0.0, step = 0.1, group = grpStrat, tooltip = "Position -> flat (normal exit) once |z| falls back below this level.")
stopZ       = input.float(3.5, "Hard Stop Threshold |z|", minval = 0.1, step = 0.1, group = grpStrat, tooltip = "Position -> flat (flagged as a STOP, not a normal exit) if |z| ever exceeds this level -- the mean-reversion premise is treated as broken.")

// See INTRADAY ACCOMMODATION in the header for why these exist. Both
// default on, since off the Daily chart they fix the specific artifact
// that produced ~220 trades / ~18% win rate during testing.
useSessionFilter = input.bool(true, "Restrict Signals to Regular Trading Hours (NYSE)", group = grpSession, tooltip = "Ignored on the Daily chart. On intraday timeframes, only evaluates entries/exits/stops while the basket stocks are actively trading (09:30-16:00 by default) -- outside that window their price is stale/forward-filled while NQ keeps moving, which is what corrupts the spread.")
sessionWindow     = input.session("0930-1600", "RTH Session", group = grpSession)
sessionTZ         = input.string("America/New_York", "Session Timezone", group = grpSession)
autoScaleWindows  = input.bool(true, "Auto-Scale Hedge/Z-Score Windows for Intraday", group = grpSession, tooltip = "Ignored on the Daily chart. Stretches Hedge Ratio Window and Z-Score Lookback by the chart's estimated bars-per-RTH-day, so '90' still spans roughly 90 TRADING DAYS of memory instead of collapsing to a few hours of 5-minute bars.")

// Position sizing and a hard loss governor for a prop-firm-style account
// where breaching a max loss / trailing drawdown limit ends the account.
// This sizes and tracks the NQ LEG only (the actually-fundable/tradable
// side of the pair at a prop firm -- see the companion strategy file's
// identical NQ-leg-only limitation), using the live implied stop price
// from the NQ PRICE PROJECTION block. It is a PLANNING/MONITORING aid, not
// an execution guarantee: a real stop order can still gap past its price,
// and this script cannot enforce anything on your broker/prop firm
// platform -- always place a real protective stop order sized the same way.
maxLossUSD      = input.float(2000, "Max Account Loss ($)", minval = 1, group = grpRisk, tooltip = "Hard ceiling on cumulative NQ-leg losses (e.g. a prop firm's max loss / trailing drawdown rule). Once hit, new entries are blocked and the chart flags TRADING HALTED.")
riskPerTradePct = input.float(10, "Risk Per Trade (% of Max Loss)", minval = 1, maxval = 100, step = 1, group = grpRisk, tooltip = "Caps a single trade's stop-loss risk at this fraction of Max Account Loss, e.g. 10% of $2000 = $200 risked per trade -- deliberately conservative, since this pair lost money net of costs even in its best backtest segment.")
contractType    = input.string("MNQ - Micro ($2/pt)", "Contract Type", options = ["MNQ - Micro ($2/pt)", "NQ - Full ($20/pt)"], group = grpRisk, tooltip = "Dollar value per NQ index point per contract, used for position sizing and P&L tracking below. Micro (MNQ) gives much finer position-size granularity on a small loss budget.")
pointValue      = contractType == "NQ - Full ($20/pt)" ? 20.0 : 2.0

showTable   = input.bool(true, "Show Stats Table", group = grpDisplay)
showMarkers = input.bool(true, "Show Entry/Exit/Stop Markers", group = grpDisplay)
showBg      = input.bool(true, "Show Position Background Shading", group = grpDisplay)
showLevels  = input.bool(true, "Show Price Reference Bands on NQ Chart (always on, not just while in a trade)", group = grpDisplay)
showHistory = input.bool(true, "Show Trade History Table", group = grpDisplay)
historyRows = input.int(10, "Trade History Rows (most recent first)", minval = 1, maxval = 20, group = grpDisplay)

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// TIMEFRAME CHECK + INTRADAY ACCOMMODATION -- every default parameter was
// tuned on DAILY bars. See the header's INTRADAY ACCOMMODATION note for the
// two specific fixes applied below (RTH session filter, window rescaling).
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// RTH session gate: on intraday timeframes, non-na only while the basket
// stocks are actually trading. Always true on the Daily chart (each daily
// bar already only forms from a full trading session, so the concept
// doesn't apply there -- time()'s intraday session check isn't meaningful
// against a daily bar's own open time).
inRTH     = not na(time(timeframe.period, sessionWindow, sessionTZ))
sessionOK = timeframe.isdaily or timeframe.isweekly or timeframe.ismonthly or not useSessionFilter or inRTH

// Window rescaling: stretch hedgeWindow/lookbackZ by the chart's estimated
// bars-per-RTH-day (390 minutes = one 6.5-hour NYSE session) so they still
// span roughly the same number of TRADING DAYS as intended, not just "90
// bars" of whatever the chart's native resolution happens to be.
// CLAMPED to maxTaLength: Pine's ta.* functions hard-cap how many
// historical bars a length argument can reference at 5000 (confirmed by
// TradingView's own compile error on unclamped values -- "references too
// many historical candles"). That cap applies PER CALL, but zscore's own
// effLookbackZ-bar window operates on `spread`, which only starts
// producing values once `beta` (itself an effHedgeWindow-bar rolling
// calc) first warms up -- so the z-score's REAL warm-up requirement is
// effHedgeWindow + effLookbackZ bars of history combined, confirmed by
// testing: beta read a valid 0.8058 on a 5-minute chart while z-score
// still showed na, because 4500+4500=9000 bars weren't yet loaded. Each
// window is kept well under half the hard cap so the combined total stays
// comfortably inside what's actually available. On very fine timeframes
// (e.g. 1-minute) this means the window ends up considerably shorter than
// a true 90-trading-day equivalent -- still far better than the unscaled
// 90 raw bars, just not a full match.
maxTaLength    = 2000
minutesPerBar  = timeframe.in_seconds(timeframe.period) / 60
barsPerRTHDay  = minutesPerBar > 0 ? math.max(1, math.round(390 / minutesPerBar)) : 1
scaleWindows   = autoScaleWindows and not timeframe.isdaily and not timeframe.isweekly and not timeframe.ismonthly
effHedgeWindow = scaleWindows ? math.min(maxTaLength, hedgeWindow * barsPerRTHDay) : hedgeWindow
effLookbackZ   = scaleWindows ? math.min(maxTaLength, lookbackZ   * barsPerRTHDay) : lookbackZ
windowClamped  = scaleWindows and (hedgeWindow * barsPerRTHDay > maxTaLength or lookbackZ * barsPerRTHDay > maxTaLength)

// Both intraday mitigations on = a softer informational note. Either one
// off (or on Daily, where neither applies/matters) = no note needed if
// Daily, or the original loud alarm if intraday and unmitigated.
intradayAdapted = not timeframe.isdaily and useSessionFilter and autoScaleWindows
showTfAlarm      = not timeframe.isdaily and not intradayAdapted

var label tfWarnLbl = na
if barstate.islast
    label.delete(tfWarnLbl)
    if showTfAlarm
        tfWarnLbl := label.new(bar_index, na, text = "⚠ WRONG TIMEFRAME\nTuned for the DAILY chart -- current: " + timeframe.period + "\nEnable RTH Session Filter + Auto-Scale Windows\n(Intraday Accommodation group) or switch to Daily.", style = label.style_label_down, yloc = yloc.abovebar, color = color.new(color.orange, 0), textcolor = color.black, size = size.large, force_overlay = true)
    else if intradayAdapted
        tfWarnLbl := label.new(bar_index, na, text = "ℹ INTRADAY MODE (" + timeframe.period + "): RTH-filtered, windows auto-scaled to ~" + str.tostring(effHedgeWindow) + " bars" + (windowClamped ? " (capped at Pine's 5000-bar ta.* limit -- shorter than a full 90-trading-day equivalent)" : "") + ".\nStill not independently validated intraday -- see header.", style = label.style_label_down, yloc = yloc.abovebar, color = color.new(color.blue, 0), textcolor = color.white, size = size.normal, force_overlay = true)

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// BASKET CONSTRUCTION -- equal-weighted top-10 Nasdaq-100 mega-cap index
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Each constituent's daily close is pulled non-repainting (lookahead_off).
// All 10 basket legs are always fetched, even when the user has selected a
// non-basket partner below -- Pine's request.security() calls live at
// global scope and are cheapest/most reliable when called unconditionally
// every bar rather than branched on an input; 11 total security() calls
// (10 legs + 1 single-symbol fetch) is well under Pine's per-script limit.
c1  = request.security(sym1,  timeframe.period, close, lookahead = barmerge.lookahead_off)
c2  = request.security(sym2,  timeframe.period, close, lookahead = barmerge.lookahead_off)
c3  = request.security(sym3,  timeframe.period, close, lookahead = barmerge.lookahead_off)
c4  = request.security(sym4,  timeframe.period, close, lookahead = barmerge.lookahead_off)
c5  = request.security(sym5,  timeframe.period, close, lookahead = barmerge.lookahead_off)
c6  = request.security(sym6,  timeframe.period, close, lookahead = barmerge.lookahead_off)
c7  = request.security(sym7,  timeframe.period, close, lookahead = barmerge.lookahead_off)
c8  = request.security(sym8,  timeframe.period, close, lookahead = barmerge.lookahead_off)
c9  = request.security(sym9,  timeframe.period, close, lookahead = barmerge.lookahead_off)
c10 = request.security(sym10, timeframe.period, close, lookahead = barmerge.lookahead_off)

// Normalizes a price series to 1.0 at the first bar of data this call site
// ever sees. Pine has no reliable way to react to pure chart pan/zoom
// (visible range) without new data arriving, so "first bar in view" is
// implemented as "first loaded bar" -- deterministic and repaint-free,
// per the spec's own "(or at indicator load)" fallback. Each of the 10
// call sites below (f_normalize(c1) .. f_normalize(c10)) keeps its own
// independent `base`, since persistent `var` state in Pine is scoped per
// textual call site.
f_normalize(src) =>
    var float base = na
    if na(base) and not na(src)
        base := src
    na(base) ? na : src / base

n1  = f_normalize(c1)
n2  = f_normalize(c2)
n3  = f_normalize(c3)
n4  = f_normalize(c4)
n5  = f_normalize(c5)
n6  = f_normalize(c6)
n7  = f_normalize(c7)
n8  = f_normalize(c8)
n9  = f_normalize(c9)
n10 = f_normalize(c10)

// Equal-weight average of the 10 normalized legs, rebased to start at 1000.
basketRaw = (n1 + n2 + n3 + n4 + n5 + n6 + n7 + n8 + n9 + n10) / 10
basket    = basketRaw * 1000

// Single-symbol partner fetch, used whenever the user picks something other
// than the basket.
singleTicker = partnerChoice == "QQQ" ? "QQQ" : partnerChoice == "ES1!" ? "ES1!" : partnerChoice == "XLK" ? "XLK" : "QQQ"
singleClose  = request.security(singleTicker, timeframe.period, close, lookahead = barmerge.lookahead_off)

partner = partnerChoice == "Equal-Weight Basket" ? basket : singleClose

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// BETA / SPREAD / Z-SCORE -- derived on log-prices of NQ (chart symbol) and partner
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Pine has no built-in rolling OLS, so the rolling hedge ratio (beta) is
// derived algebraically from rolling correlation and rolling stdev:
//   beta = corr(logPartner, logNQ) * stdev(logPartner) / stdev(logNQ)
// which is exactly the slope coefficient of a simple OLS regression of
// logPartner on logNQ, evaluated on a rolling window.
logNQ      = math.log(close)
logPartner = math.log(partner)

// Uses effHedgeWindow/effLookbackZ (see TIMEFRAME CHECK + INTRADAY
// ACCOMMODATION above), not the raw input -- on the Daily chart these are
// identical to hedgeWindow/lookbackZ; on intraday charts with Auto-Scale
// enabled they're stretched to span roughly the same number of trading
// days instead of a much shorter, noisier window of raw intraday bars.
beta = ta.correlation(logPartner, logNQ, effHedgeWindow) * ta.stdev(logPartner, effHedgeWindow) / ta.stdev(logNQ, effHedgeWindow)

// Spread = the hedged log-price differential. If partner and NQ stay
// cointegrated, this spread should mean-revert around its own rolling mean.
spread = logPartner - beta * logNQ

// Z-score of the spread against its own rolling mean/stdev -- this is the
// core mean-reversion signal. spreadMean/spreadStdev are named separately
// (rather than inlined) so the NQ PRICE PROJECTION block below can reuse
// the exact same rolling stats the z-score itself was built from.
spreadMean  = ta.sma(spread, effLookbackZ)
spreadStdev = ta.stdev(spread, effLookbackZ)
zscore      = (spread - spreadMean) / spreadStdev

// --- Lag by exactly one bar for every trading decision (see header) -------
// zSig/betaSig/spreadSig are the values as of the previous, fully confirmed
// bar. All entry/exit/stop logic below reads ONLY these lagged series, so a
// decision made "on" bar T was actually knowable at the open of bar T and
// cannot repaint intrabar.
zSig      = zscore[1]
spreadSig = spread[1]

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// NQ PRICE PROJECTION -- inverts the z-score formula to express a target
// z-value as an implied NQ price, so entry/TP/SL can be drawn directly on
// the NQ candles instead of only in z-score units.
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Solving zscore = (logPartner - beta*logNQ - spreadMean) / spreadStdev for
// logNQ, holding TODAY's partner price/beta/mean/stdev fixed, gives the NQ
// log-price at which the z-score would equal zTarget right now:
//   logNQ = (logPartner - spreadMean - zTarget * spreadStdev) / beta
// This is a LIVE ESTIMATE, not a fixed target: beta/spreadMean/spreadStdev
// recompute every bar (exactly like the real exit/stop condition, which is
// also re-evaluated from live data, not frozen at entry), so the projected
// price will drift over the life of a trade. That's intentional -- a frozen
// snapshot would quietly drift out of sync with the actual, live z-score
// condition that really triggers the exit/stop.
//
// GUARD: when beta is small (or spreadStdev is large relative to it, which
// happens on timeframes/data this wasn't tuned for -- see the TIMEFRAME
// CHECK below), the exponent can blow up and math.exp() overflows toward
// (+/-)infinity. Instead of silently plotting/displaying a meaningless huge
// number, treat any result more than 5x away from the current NQ price as
// unreliable and return na (which simply doesn't plot / shows blank).
f_impliedNQPrice(zTarget) =>
    raw = math.exp((logPartner - spreadMean - zTarget * spreadStdev) / beta)
    raw > close / 5 and raw < close * 5 ? raw : na

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// STATE MACHINE -- var int posState: 0 = flat, 1 = long spread, -1 = short spread
// (named posState, not "position", because Pine reserves the identifier
// `position` as a built-in namespace -- position.top_right etc. -- used just
// below by table.new(); reusing it as a variable name would shadow that
// namespace and break the table's position argument.)
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Long spread  = long partner, short beta*NQ  (entered when z falls below -entryZ)
// Short spread = short partner, long beta*NQ  (entered when z rises above +entryZ)
// Exit-to-flat = normal exit once |z| reverts back inside the exit band
// Stop-out     = forced flat if |z| blows THROUGH the stop band -- the
//                mean-reversion premise is treated as invalidated, and this
//                is tracked/plotted separately from a normal exit.
var int   posState      = 0
var float entrySpread   = na
var int   wins          = 0
var int   losses        = 0
var int   closedTrades  = 0

// Entry / take-profit / stop-loss levels for the CURRENTLY OPEN trade, in
// z-score units. Z-score units, not an NQ dollar price, because that's the
// actual variable this strategy trades against -- beta and the spread's
// rolling mean/stdev shift every bar, so a fixed NQ price target/stop isn't
// well-defined the way a simple ATR offset would be. Take-profit is the
// near edge of the exit band (where the spread has reverted as expected);
// stop-loss is the stop band (where the spread has instead blown further
// against the trade).
var float entryLevel = na
var float tpLevel     = na
var float slLevel     = na
var label entryLbl    = na
var label tpLbl        = na
var label slLbl        = na

// Same three levels, but on the NQ price axis so they show up directly on
// the NQ candles (see NQ PRICE PROJECTION above). entryPriceNQ is a fixed,
// historical fact (the actual NQ close when the trade was opened); tpPriceNQ
// / slPriceNQ are the LIVE-updating implied price via f_impliedNQPrice(), so
// they are computed fresh every bar below rather than stored as `var`.
var float entryPriceNQ = na
var label entryPriceLbl = na

// Position-sizing / loss-governor state (see Prop Firm Risk Management
// inputs above). slPriceAtEntry/tradeContracts are FROZEN at entry -- the
// size of a trade, once taken, doesn't change; only the live TP/SL display
// levels (tpPriceNQ/slPriceNQ below) keep drifting. cumPnlUSD is the
// running realized NQ-leg P&L across all closed trades, and killSwitch
// latches permanently true once it breaches -maxLossUSD (a real prop-firm
// breach doesn't reset itself, so neither does this).
var float slPriceAtEntry = na
var int   tradeContracts = 0
var float cumPnlUSD      = 0.0
var bool  killSwitch     = false

// Rolling trade history log (see TRADE HISTORY TABLE below): the last
// `historyRows` closed trades, oldest dropped via array.shift() once the
// cap is exceeded. Declared with `var` so the arrays persist and mutate
// across bars rather than being recreated (and emptied) every bar.
var array<string> histDir       = array.new_string(0)
var array<float>  histEntryPx   = array.new_float(0)
var array<float>  histExitPx    = array.new_float(0)
var array<int>    histContracts = array.new_int(0)
var array<float>  histPnlUSD    = array.new_float(0)
var array<string> histResult    = array.new_string(0)

// sessionOK (see TIMEFRAME CHECK + INTRADAY ACCOMMODATION above) gates
// EVERY decision -- entries, exits, and stops -- so a position already
// open when the stock market closes simply holds, untouched by the
// artificial spread swings that stale forward-filled stock data would
// otherwise create, until RTH resumes.
haveSignal = not na(zSig) and sessionOK

// Prospective stop distance / position size for EACH possible direction,
// evaluated every bar with live data -- BEFORE any entry decision -- so a
// trade whose stop is too wide to size even 1 micro contract within the
// per-trade risk budget is skipped entirely rather than forced through.
riskBudgetUSD = maxLossUSD * riskPerTradePct / 100

prospSlLong  = f_impliedNQPrice(-stopZ)
prospSlShort = f_impliedNQPrice(stopZ)
prospDistLong  = na(prospSlLong)  ? na : math.abs(close - prospSlLong)
prospDistShort = na(prospSlShort) ? na : math.abs(close - prospSlShort)
prospContractsLong  = (not na(prospDistLong)  and prospDistLong  > 0) ? math.floor(riskBudgetUSD / (prospDistLong  * pointValue)) : 0
prospContractsShort = (not na(prospDistShort) and prospDistShort > 0) ? math.floor(riskBudgetUSD / (prospDistShort * pointValue)) : 0

longEntrySig  = haveSignal and not killSwitch and posState == 0 and zSig < -entryZ and prospContractsLong  >= 1
shortEntrySig = haveSignal and not killSwitch and posState == 0 and zSig >  entryZ and prospContractsShort >= 1
stopSig       = haveSignal and posState != 0 and math.abs(zSig) > stopZ
exitSig       = haveSignal and posState != 0 and not stopSig and math.abs(zSig) < exitZ

if longEntrySig
    // Long spread = long partner, short beta*NQ -> the NQ leg itself is short.
    posState       := 1
    entrySpread    := spreadSig
    entryLevel     := zSig
    tpLevel        := -exitZ
    slLevel        := -stopZ
    entryPriceNQ   := close
    slPriceAtEntry := prospSlLong
    tradeContracts := prospContractsLong
    entryLbl := label.new(bar_index, zSig,   text = "ENTRY " + str.tostring(zSig, "#.##"), style = label.style_triangleup,  yloc = yloc.price, color = color.new(color.green, 0), textcolor = color.white, size = size.small)
    tpLbl    := label.new(bar_index, tpLevel, text = "TP " + str.tostring(tpLevel, "#.##"), style = label.style_label_left, color = color.new(color.teal, 20), textcolor = color.white, size = size.tiny)
    slLbl    := label.new(bar_index, slLevel, text = "SL " + str.tostring(slLevel, "#.##"), style = label.style_label_left, color = color.new(color.red, 20),  textcolor = color.white, size = size.tiny)
    entryPriceLbl := label.new(bar_index, na, text = "LONG SPREAD ENTRY\n" + str.tostring(close, format.mintick) + "\n" + str.tostring(tradeContracts) + " contract(s)", style = label.style_triangleup, yloc = yloc.belowbar, color = color.new(color.green, 0), textcolor = color.white, size = size.small, force_overlay = true)

if shortEntrySig
    // Short spread = short partner, long beta*NQ -> the NQ leg itself is long.
    posState       := -1
    entrySpread    := spreadSig
    entryLevel     := zSig
    tpLevel        := exitZ
    slLevel        := stopZ
    entryPriceNQ   := close
    slPriceAtEntry := prospSlShort
    tradeContracts := prospContractsShort
    entryLbl := label.new(bar_index, zSig,   text = "ENTRY " + str.tostring(zSig, "#.##"), style = label.style_triangledown, yloc = yloc.price, color = color.new(color.red, 0),  textcolor = color.white, size = size.small)
    tpLbl    := label.new(bar_index, tpLevel, text = "TP " + str.tostring(tpLevel, "#.##"), style = label.style_label_left,  color = color.new(color.teal, 20), textcolor = color.white, size = size.tiny)
    slLbl    := label.new(bar_index, slLevel, text = "SL " + str.tostring(slLevel, "#.##"), style = label.style_label_left,  color = color.new(color.red, 20),  textcolor = color.white, size = size.tiny)
    entryPriceLbl := label.new(bar_index, na, text = "SHORT SPREAD ENTRY\n" + str.tostring(close, format.mintick) + "\n" + str.tostring(tradeContracts) + " contract(s)", style = label.style_triangledown, yloc = yloc.abovebar, color = color.new(color.red, 0), textcolor = color.white, size = size.small, force_overlay = true)

if stopSig or exitSig
    // Spread P&L: a long-spread trade profits when the spread rose from
    // entry to exit; a short-spread trade profits when it fell.
    tradePnl = posState == 1 ? (spreadSig - entrySpread) : (entrySpread - spreadSig)
    if tradePnl > 0
        wins += 1
    else
        losses += 1
    closedTrades += 1
    // Real $ P&L on the NQ leg at the size actually sized for this trade --
    // posState == 1 (long spread) means the NQ leg is SHORT, so it profits
    // when NQ price FALLS; posState == -1 (short spread) means the NQ leg
    // is LONG, so it profits when NQ price RISES.
    exitPriceNQ = close
    pnlPts = posState == 1 ? (entryPriceNQ - exitPriceNQ) : (exitPriceNQ - entryPriceNQ)
    tradePnlUSD = pnlPts * pointValue * tradeContracts
    cumPnlUSD += tradePnlUSD
    killSwitch := killSwitch or (cumPnlUSD <= -maxLossUSD)

    // Log this trade into the rolling history (see TRADE HISTORY TABLE
    // below) before the fields below get reset to na/0.
    array.push(histDir, posState == 1 ? "Long" : "Short")
    array.push(histEntryPx, entryPriceNQ)
    array.push(histExitPx, exitPriceNQ)
    array.push(histContracts, tradeContracts)
    array.push(histPnlUSD, tradePnlUSD)
    array.push(histResult, stopSig ? "STOP" : (tradePnlUSD > 0 ? "WIN" : "LOSS"))
    if array.size(histDir) > historyRows
        array.shift(histDir)
        array.shift(histEntryPx)
        array.shift(histExitPx)
        array.shift(histContracts)
        array.shift(histPnlUSD)
        array.shift(histResult)

    posState       := 0
    entrySpread    := na
    entryLevel     := na
    tpLevel        := na
    slLevel        := na
    entryPriceNQ   := na
    slPriceAtEntry := na
    tradeContracts := 0
    label.delete(entryLbl)
    label.delete(tpLbl)
    label.delete(slLbl)
    label.delete(entryPriceLbl)
    entryLbl := na
    tpLbl    := na
    slLbl    := na
    entryPriceLbl := na

// Live-updating TP/SL implied NQ price for the open trade (see NQ PRICE
// PROJECTION above) -- recomputed every bar, not stored in `var`.
tpPriceNQ = posState == 1 ? f_impliedNQPrice(-exitZ) : posState == -1 ? f_impliedNQPrice(exitZ) : na
slPriceNQ = posState == 1 ? f_impliedNQPrice(-stopZ) : posState == -1 ? f_impliedNQPrice(stopZ) : na

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// TRADING HALTED WARNING -- fires once killSwitch latches true (cumulative
// NQ-leg loss reached -maxLossUSD). Mirrors the TIMEFRAME CHECK warning
// above: loud and impossible to miss, since new entries are blocked from
// here on for the rest of the chart's life.
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
var label haltLbl = na
if killSwitch and barstate.islast
    label.delete(haltLbl)
    haltLbl := label.new(bar_index, na, text = "🛑 MAX LOSS HIT -- TRADING HALTED\nCumulative NQ P&L: $" + str.tostring(cumPnlUSD, "#.##"), style = label.style_label_down, yloc = yloc.abovebar, color = color.new(color.red, 0), textcolor = color.white, size = size.large, force_overlay = true)
bgcolor(killSwitch ? color.new(color.red, 85) : na, title = "Trading Halted Shading", force_overlay = true)

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// PLOTS -- z-score line + reference bands (own pane, since overlay = false)
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
plot(zscore, title = "Z-Score", color = color.new(color.blue, 0), linewidth = 2)

hline(0,        "Zero",           color = color.gray,                 linestyle = hline.style_solid)
hline(entryZ,   "Entry +",        color = color.new(color.orange, 0), linestyle = hline.style_dashed)
hline(-entryZ,  "Entry -",        color = color.new(color.orange, 0), linestyle = hline.style_dashed)
hline(exitZ,    "Exit +",         color = color.new(color.teal, 0),   linestyle = hline.style_dotted)
hline(-exitZ,   "Exit -",         color = color.new(color.teal, 0),   linestyle = hline.style_dotted)
hline(stopZ,    "Stop +",         color = color.new(color.red, 0),    linestyle = hline.style_solid)
hline(-stopZ,   "Stop -",         color = color.new(color.red, 0),    linestyle = hline.style_solid)

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// OPEN-TRADE ENTRY / TAKE-PROFIT / STOP-LOSS LEVELS
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Step-lines that trace the current trade's TP/SL bands (z-score units --
// see the state-machine comment above for why not an NQ dollar price).
// plot.style_linebr breaks the line whenever the value is na, so each trade
// gets its own clearly bounded pair of levels that vanish once flat again --
// same convention as the SL/TP plots in Momentum_ROC_Signal_ES_NQ.pine.
plot(posState != 0 ? entryLevel : na, title = "Entry Level (z)",       color = color.new(color.gray, 40), style = plot.style_linebr, linewidth = 1)
plot(posState != 0 ? tpLevel    : na, title = "Take Profit Level (z)", color = color.new(color.teal, 0),  style = plot.style_linebr, linewidth = 2)
plot(posState != 0 ? slLevel    : na, title = "Stop Loss Level (z)",   color = color.new(color.red, 0),   style = plot.style_linebr, linewidth = 2)

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// PLOTS -- same entry/TP/SL levels, on the NQ price axis, pushed onto the
// main chart via force_overlay so they appear directly on the NQ candles.
// entryPriceNQ is the actual historical fill price (fixed); tpPriceNQ/
// slPriceNQ are the live implied-price estimate and will visibly shift bar
// to bar as beta/spreadMean/spreadStdev update (see NQ PRICE PROJECTION).
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
plot(posState != 0 ? entryPriceNQ : na, title = "Entry Price (NQ)",            color = color.new(color.gray, 40), style = plot.style_linebr, linewidth = 1, force_overlay = true)
plot(posState != 0 ? tpPriceNQ    : na, title = "Take Profit (NQ, live est.)", color = color.new(color.teal, 0),  style = plot.style_linebr, linewidth = 2, force_overlay = true)
plot(posState != 0 ? slPriceNQ    : na, title = "Stop Loss (NQ, live est.)",   color = color.new(color.red, 0),   style = plot.style_linebr, linewidth = 2, force_overlay = true)

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// ALWAYS-ON PRICE REFERENCE BANDS -- the entry/exit/stop lines above only
// draw while a trade is actually open (posState != 0), so on a quiet chart
// with no active signal, NOTHING shows on the NQ candles even though the
// indicator is working correctly. These six bands are the SAME z-score
// thresholds (+-entryZ, +-exitZ, +-stopZ) projected onto the NQ price axis
// via f_impliedNQPrice(), plotted continuously regardless of position state
// -- i.e. "if NQ traded to about this price today, here's roughly which
// threshold it would cross." They are thin/unlabeled-by-direction because,
// while flat, "take profit" and "stop loss" aren't yet meaningful (that
// depends on which side you'd enter); once a trade opens, the bold
// Entry/Take Profit/Stop Loss lines plotted just above are the SAME values,
// specifically labeled for the side that's actually open.
entryHiPriceNQ = f_impliedNQPrice(entryZ)
entryLoPriceNQ = f_impliedNQPrice(-entryZ)
exitHiPriceNQ  = f_impliedNQPrice(exitZ)
exitLoPriceNQ  = f_impliedNQPrice(-exitZ)
stopHiPriceNQ  = f_impliedNQPrice(stopZ)
stopLoPriceNQ  = f_impliedNQPrice(-stopZ)

plot(showLevels ? entryHiPriceNQ : na, title = "Entry Threshold + (NQ)", color = color.new(color.orange, 40), style = plot.style_line, linewidth = 1, force_overlay = true)
plot(showLevels ? entryLoPriceNQ : na, title = "Entry Threshold - (NQ)", color = color.new(color.orange, 40), style = plot.style_line, linewidth = 1, force_overlay = true)
plot(showLevels ? exitHiPriceNQ  : na, title = "Exit Threshold + (NQ)",  color = color.new(color.teal, 55),   style = plot.style_line, linewidth = 1, force_overlay = true)
plot(showLevels ? exitLoPriceNQ  : na, title = "Exit Threshold - (NQ)",  color = color.new(color.teal, 55),   style = plot.style_line, linewidth = 1, force_overlay = true)
plot(showLevels ? stopHiPriceNQ  : na, title = "Stop Threshold + (NQ)",  color = color.new(color.red, 55),    style = plot.style_line, linewidth = 1, force_overlay = true)
plot(showLevels ? stopLoPriceNQ  : na, title = "Stop Threshold - (NQ)",  color = color.new(color.red, 55),    style = plot.style_line, linewidth = 1, force_overlay = true)

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// BACKGROUND SHADING -- current position state, in both the z-score pane and
// (via force_overlay) tinted onto the main price chart too.
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
bgCol = posState == 1 ? color.new(color.green, 90) : posState == -1 ? color.new(color.red, 90) : na
bgcolor(showBg ? bgCol : na, title = "Position State Shading (pane)")
bgcolor(showBg ? bgCol : na, title = "Position State Shading (chart)", force_overlay = true)

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// SIGNAL MARKERS -- plotted at the z-score value itself via location.absolute
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
plotshape(showMarkers and longEntrySig  ? zscore : na, title = "Long Spread Entry",  style = shape.triangleup,   location = location.absolute, color = color.new(color.green, 0),  size = size.small)
plotshape(showMarkers and shortEntrySig ? zscore : na, title = "Short Spread Entry", style = shape.triangledown, location = location.absolute, color = color.new(color.red, 0),    size = size.small)
plotshape(showMarkers and exitSig       ? zscore : na, title = "Exit to Flat",       style = shape.xcross,       location = location.absolute, color = color.new(color.gray, 0),   size = size.small)
plotshape(showMarkers and stopSig       ? zscore : na, title = "Stop-Out",           style = shape.diamond,      location = location.absolute, color = color.new(color.red, 0),    size = size.normal)

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// STATS TABLE -- top-right, pinned onto the main chart via force_overlay
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
var table infoTable = table.new(position.top_right, 2, 13, border_width = 1, force_overlay = true)

if showTable and barstate.islast
    posText = posState == 1 ? "LONG SPREAD" : posState == -1 ? "SHORT SPREAD" : "FLAT"
    winRate = closedTrades > 0 ? (wins / closedTrades) * 100.0 : na
    budgetLeftUSD = math.max(0.0, maxLossUSD + cumPnlUSD)
    riskTableColor = killSwitch ? color.new(color.red, 60) : color.new(color.gray, 80)

    // na(zscore)/na(beta) show as "Warming up..." rather than the raw
    // "NaN" that str.tostring() prints for an na float -- both need
    // effHedgeWindow (+ effLookbackZ for zscore, see TIMEFRAME CHECK +
    // INTRADAY ACCOMMODATION above) bars of history before they're valid.
    table.cell(infoTable, 0, 0, "Z-Score",       bgcolor = color.new(color.gray, 80), text_color = color.white)
    table.cell(infoTable, 1, 0, na(zscore) ? "Warming up..." : str.tostring(zscore, "#.##"), bgcolor = color.new(color.gray, 80), text_color = color.white)
    table.cell(infoTable, 0, 1, "Beta",          bgcolor = color.new(color.gray, 80), text_color = color.white)
    table.cell(infoTable, 1, 1, na(beta) ? "Warming up..." : str.tostring(beta, "#.####"), bgcolor = color.new(color.gray, 80), text_color = color.white)
    table.cell(infoTable, 0, 2, "Position",       bgcolor = color.new(color.gray, 80), text_color = color.white)
    table.cell(infoTable, 1, 2, killSwitch ? "HALTED" : posText, bgcolor = color.new(color.gray, 80), text_color = killSwitch ? color.red : posState == 1 ? color.lime : posState == -1 ? color.red : color.white)
    // Always-on prospective entry thresholds (see ALWAYS-ON PRICE REFERENCE
    // BANDS above), so the table is informative even while flat.
    table.cell(infoTable, 0, 3, "Entry Threshold +", bgcolor = color.new(color.gray, 80), text_color = color.white)
    table.cell(infoTable, 1, 3, na(entryHiPriceNQ) ? "N/A" : str.tostring(entryHiPriceNQ, format.mintick), bgcolor = color.new(color.gray, 80), text_color = color.orange)
    table.cell(infoTable, 0, 4, "Entry Threshold -", bgcolor = color.new(color.gray, 80), text_color = color.white)
    table.cell(infoTable, 1, 4, na(entryLoPriceNQ) ? "N/A" : str.tostring(entryLoPriceNQ, format.mintick), bgcolor = color.new(color.gray, 80), text_color = color.orange)
    table.cell(infoTable, 0, 5, "Entry Price (NQ)", bgcolor = color.new(color.gray, 80), text_color = color.white)
    table.cell(infoTable, 1, 5, posState != 0 ? str.tostring(entryPriceNQ, format.mintick) : "-- (no open trade)", bgcolor = color.new(color.gray, 80), text_color = color.white)
    table.cell(infoTable, 0, 6, "Take Profit (NQ)", bgcolor = color.new(color.gray, 80), text_color = color.white)
    table.cell(infoTable, 1, 6, posState == 0 ? "-- (no open trade)" : na(tpPriceNQ) ? "N/A" : str.tostring(tpPriceNQ, format.mintick) + " (est.)", bgcolor = color.new(color.gray, 80), text_color = color.teal)
    table.cell(infoTable, 0, 7, "Stop Loss (NQ)", bgcolor = color.new(color.gray, 80), text_color = color.white)
    table.cell(infoTable, 1, 7, posState == 0 ? "-- (no open trade)" : na(slPriceNQ) ? "N/A" : str.tostring(slPriceNQ, format.mintick) + " (est.)", bgcolor = color.new(color.gray, 80), text_color = color.red)
    // Position sizing / risk governor (see Prop Firm Risk Management inputs).
    table.cell(infoTable, 0, 8, "Contracts (" + (contractType == "NQ - Full ($20/pt)" ? "NQ" : "MNQ") + ")", bgcolor = riskTableColor, text_color = color.white)
    table.cell(infoTable, 1, 8, posState != 0 ? str.tostring(tradeContracts) : "--", bgcolor = riskTableColor, text_color = color.white)
    table.cell(infoTable, 0, 9, "Cumulative P&L ($)", bgcolor = riskTableColor, text_color = color.white)
    table.cell(infoTable, 1, 9, "$" + str.tostring(cumPnlUSD, "#.##"), bgcolor = riskTableColor, text_color = cumPnlUSD >= 0 ? color.lime : color.red)
    table.cell(infoTable, 0, 10, "Loss Budget Left", bgcolor = riskTableColor, text_color = color.white)
    table.cell(infoTable, 1, 10, killSwitch ? "$0 -- HALTED" : "$" + str.tostring(budgetLeftUSD, "#.##"), bgcolor = riskTableColor, text_color = killSwitch ? color.red : color.white)
    table.cell(infoTable, 0, 11, "Closed Trades",  bgcolor = color.new(color.gray, 80), text_color = color.white)
    table.cell(infoTable, 1, 11, str.tostring(closedTrades), bgcolor = color.new(color.gray, 80), text_color = color.white)
    table.cell(infoTable, 0, 12, "Win Rate",       bgcolor = color.new(color.gray, 80), text_color = color.white)
    table.cell(infoTable, 1, 12, na(winRate) ? "n/a" : str.tostring(winRate, "#.#") + "%", bgcolor = color.new(color.gray, 80), text_color = color.white)

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// TRADE HISTORY TABLE -- bottom-right (opposite corner from the stats
// table above), pinned onto the main chart via force_overlay. Shows the
// last `historyRows` closed trades, most recent first, from the rolling
// log built up in the state machine's closing block. Green background =
// winning trade, red = losing trade or stop-out (labeled distinctly).
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
var table historyTable = table.new(position.bottom_right, 6, historyRows + 1, border_width = 1, force_overlay = true)

if showHistory and barstate.islast
    table.cell(historyTable, 0, 0, "#",       bgcolor = color.new(color.gray, 60), text_color = color.white)
    table.cell(historyTable, 1, 0, "Dir",     bgcolor = color.new(color.gray, 60), text_color = color.white)
    table.cell(historyTable, 2, 0, "Qty",     bgcolor = color.new(color.gray, 60), text_color = color.white)
    table.cell(historyTable, 3, 0, "Entry",   bgcolor = color.new(color.gray, 60), text_color = color.white)
    table.cell(historyTable, 4, 0, "Exit",    bgcolor = color.new(color.gray, 60), text_color = color.white)
    table.cell(historyTable, 5, 0, "P&L ($)", bgcolor = color.new(color.gray, 60), text_color = color.white)

    histCount = array.size(histDir)
    for i = 0 to historyRows - 1
        rowIdx = histCount - 1 - i  // most recent trade first
        if rowIdx >= 0
            dir       = array.get(histDir, rowIdx)
            qty       = array.get(histContracts, rowIdx)
            entryPx   = array.get(histEntryPx, rowIdx)
            exitPx    = array.get(histExitPx, rowIdx)
            pnlUSD    = array.get(histPnlUSD, rowIdx)
            result    = array.get(histResult, rowIdx)
            rowColor = result == "STOP" ? color.new(color.red, 65) : pnlUSD > 0 ? color.new(color.green, 80) : color.new(color.red, 80)
            table.cell(historyTable, 0, i + 1, str.tostring(rowIdx + 1),          bgcolor = rowColor, text_color = color.white)
            table.cell(historyTable, 1, i + 1, dir,                              bgcolor = rowColor, text_color = color.white)
            table.cell(historyTable, 2, i + 1, str.tostring(qty),                bgcolor = rowColor, text_color = color.white)
            table.cell(historyTable, 3, i + 1, str.tostring(entryPx, format.mintick), bgcolor = rowColor, text_color = color.white)
            table.cell(historyTable, 4, i + 1, str.tostring(exitPx, format.mintick),  bgcolor = rowColor, text_color = color.white)
            table.cell(historyTable, 5, i + 1, (pnlUSD >= 0 ? "+$" : "-$") + str.tostring(math.abs(pnlUSD), "#.##") + (result == "STOP" ? " STOP" : ""), bgcolor = rowColor, text_color = color.white)
        else
            table.cell(historyTable, 0, i + 1, "", bgcolor = color.new(color.gray, 90), text_color = color.white)
            table.cell(historyTable, 1, i + 1, "", bgcolor = color.new(color.gray, 90), text_color = color.white)
            table.cell(historyTable, 2, i + 1, "", bgcolor = color.new(color.gray, 90), text_color = color.white)
            table.cell(historyTable, 3, i + 1, "", bgcolor = color.new(color.gray, 90), text_color = color.white)
            table.cell(historyTable, 4, i + 1, "", bgcolor = color.new(color.gray, 90), text_color = color.white)
            table.cell(historyTable, 5, i + 1, "", bgcolor = color.new(color.gray, 90), text_color = color.white)

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// ALERTS
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
alertcondition(longEntrySig,  title = "Long Spread Entry",  message = "NQ/Basket pairs: LONG SPREAD entry (long partner, short beta*NQ) on {{ticker}} {{interval}}")
alertcondition(shortEntrySig, title = "Short Spread Entry", message = "NQ/Basket pairs: SHORT SPREAD entry (short partner, long beta*NQ) on {{ticker}} {{interval}}")
alertcondition(exitSig,       title = "Exit to Flat",       message = "NQ/Basket pairs: exit to FLAT (|z| back inside exit band) on {{ticker}} {{interval}}")
alertcondition(stopSig,       title = "Stop-Out",           message = "NQ/Basket pairs: STOPPED OUT (|z| exceeded hard stop) on {{ticker}} {{interval}}")
